    {
      "source": "hn",
      "title": "Show HN: Foolscap – a writing app that reads your book and never writes for you",
      "url": "https://foolscap.works/",
      "author": "atugard",
      "external_id": "50559749",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-11-19T13:21:20Z",
      "score": 79,
      "comments": 41,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50559749",
      "snippet": "People are scared of their ability to write and think atrophying, so I wondered: what if I built something that enhanced human creativity instead of destroying it by doing the thinking and writing for people? Foolscap is the result, or at least the first version of that idea that&#x27;s fleshed out enough to share here.<p>It reads as you write and picks out the structure: characters, ideas, threads, what&#x27;s left hanging. You can respond to what it picks out, so there&#x27;s a dialectic between the system and you as the writer. There&#x27;s also an agent on the side, plugged into the same system, so you can ask about your book and fire off actions through conversation. It never writes into your work.<p>The system can also follow theorems and proofs and string together their dependencies, but I haven&#x27;t made that a focus yet. There&#x27;s no Lean support, for example. If anyone&#x2",
      "links": []
